WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.538
Analysis starts with a useful question.

2
00:00:02.538 --> 00:00:05.226
A vague question produces vague analysis.

3
00:00:05.226 --> 00:00:07.316
If someone asks, what is going on,

4
00:00:07.316 --> 00:00:13.288
the analyst may collect a large amount of information without knowing which evidence matters.

5
00:00:13.288 --> 00:00:19.634
A stronger intelligence question identifies the decision to support, the audience, the timeframe,

6
00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:21.724
and the level of detail required.

7
00:00:21.724 --> 00:00:25.009
That structure turns curiosity into a requirement.

8
00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:28.667
An intelligence requirement should be tied to a decision.

9
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:34.639
For example, a SOC manager may need to decide whether to tune a detection, escalate an incident,

10
00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:37.550
or assign more staff to a recurring alert type.

11
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:43.448
A cloud engineer may need to decide whether a configuration pattern creates meaningful risk.

12
00:00:43.448 --> 00:00:48.226
An executive may need to decide whether a business process needs attention.

13
00:00:48.226 --> 00:00:54.571
A legal or compliance team may need a concise explanation of facts, assumptions, and response steps.

14
00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:59.797
The same information may support all of these audiences, but not in the same format.

15
00:00:59.797 --> 00:01:04.500
Audience matters because different stakeholders need different detail.

16
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.144
Technical teams may need evidence tables, timestamps, indicators, data sources, and confidence notes.

17
00:01:11.144 --> 00:01:15.997
Leaders may need key judgments, implications, options, and risk tradeoffs.

18
00:01:15.997 --> 00:01:22.865
Incident commanders may need what changed, what is known, what is uncertain, and what decision is needed now.

19
00:01:22.865 --> 00:01:26.523
A good analyst does not simply write everything they know.

20
00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:28.986
They shape the product to the decision.

21
00:01:28.986 --> 00:01:30.778
Timeframe also matters.

22
00:01:30.778 --> 00:01:35.929
A five-minute incident update is not the same as a two-week strategic assessment.

23
00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:41.080
Urgent analysis may need to state preliminary judgments and confidence clearly.

24
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:47.426
Longer analysis may include more source evaluation, alternative hypotheses, and evidence review.

25
00:01:47.426 --> 00:01:53.249
Both can be professional if the analyst is clear about limitations and avoids overclaiming.

26
00:01:53.249 --> 00:01:56.384
Clarifying the question prevents wasted effort.

27
00:01:56.384 --> 00:02:03.775
Before collecting more data, ask what decision is being supported, who will use the answer, when the answer is needed,

28
00:02:03.775 --> 00:02:10.718
what level of confidence is required, what sources are authorized, and what would make the answer actionable.

29
00:02:10.718 --> 00:02:13.405
These questions do not slow analysis down.

30
00:02:13.405 --> 00:02:18.183
They prevent the team from spending hours on information that does not help.

31
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:21.617
A strong requirement is not a bureaucratic formality.

32
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.528
It is the anchor that keeps analysis relevant.

33
00:02:24.528 --> 00:02:31.620
It helps the analyst decide what to collect, what to ignore, how to organize evidence, how much detail to include,

34
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.935
and how to communicate uncertainty.

35
00:02:33.935 --> 00:02:39.832
Good analysis begins when the question is clear enough that evidence can be judged against it.
